		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[Photo by Julie Schartner]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[To survive periods of drought, Hine’s emerald
dragonfly larvae often live in crayfish burrows (inset),
where they try to avoid becoming a meal for their hungry
roommates, the Devil crayfish. Big and Little Marsh State
Natural Area during a dry summer. Top left to right:
Big and Little Marsh State Natural Area during a wet
season; two views of the addition at Three Springs Nature
Preserve; two views of Kellner Fen Natural Area; Federally
endangered Hine’s emerald dragonfly.
Photo by Julie Schartner]]></excerpt:encoded>
